{
 "category": "31-discrete-choice-demand",
 "cards": [
  {
   "id": 266,
   "method": "BLP random-coefficients logit demand estimation",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/blp_demand.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_blp_problem",
    "dcd_blp_solve",
    "dcd_blp_shares"
   ],
   "when": "estimating demand for differentiated products from market-level shares when price is endogenous and substitution patterns must not be forced into the IIA straitjacket",
   "when_not": "you have individual choice data (-> mixed logit); there is one product; you need no substitution structure and a simple logit will do",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "nested logit",
     "criterion": "a known grouping captures substitution and you want a closed form"
    },
    {
     "alt": "mixed logit on micro data",
     "criterion": "you observe individual choices rather than market shares"
    },
    {
     "alt": "plain logit",
     "criterion": "IIA is acceptable and price is exogenous"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "beta/sigma: mean tastes and the random-coefficient dispersion",
    "xi: the structural demand shock recovered by inversion",
    "objective, gradient_norm: GMM objective and first-order condition at the optimum",
    "converged: whether BOTH the inner share inversion and the outer search converged"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the inner contraction tolerance must be far tighter than the outer one; a loose inner loop produces a smooth-looking optimum that is not one",
    "sigma is identified by variation in the choice set ACROSS markets -- with few markets it is barely identified and the standard error will not say so",
    "a reported optimum with a non-trivial gradient norm is a failed search, not a corner solution"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Berry, Levinsohn & Pakes 1995 (Econometrica 63:841-890)",
    "Nevo 2000 (Journal of Economics and Management Strategy 9:513)",
    "Conlon & Gortmaker 2020 (RAND Journal of Economics 51:1108)",
    "pyblp"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 267,
   "method": "Optimal instruments and differentiation instruments for demand systems",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/blp_instruments.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_blp_instruments",
    "dcd_optimal_instruments"
   ],
   "when": "the demand estimate is imprecise or weakly identified and you want instruments that exploit the structure of the model rather than ad hoc cost shifters",
   "when_not": "you already have strong excluded cost shifters; the model is not yet estimated at all (fit a first stage first)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "BLP instruments (sums of rival characteristics)",
     "criterion": "a cheap, transparent default"
    },
    {
     "alt": "differentiation instruments",
     "criterion": "local competition matters and the BLP sums are too coarse"
    },
    {
     "alt": "optimal instruments",
     "criterion": "you have a consistent first-stage estimate and want efficiency"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "instruments: the constructed instrument matrix",
    "first_stage_f: weak-instrument diagnostic per endogenous variable",
    "improvement: change in the GMM objective and in the standard errors"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "optimal instruments require a CONSISTENT first-stage estimate; built from a bad first stage they are optimal for the wrong model",
    "differentiation instruments are functions of characteristics, so they are invalid if characteristics are themselves endogenous",
    "a first-stage F below the conventional threshold means the whole demand estimate is untrustworthy, not merely imprecise"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Berry, Levinsohn & Pakes 1995 (Econometrica 63:841)",
    "Gandhi & Houde 2019, Measuring substitution patterns in differentiated products industries (NBER WP 26375)",
    "Chamberlain 1987 (Journal of Econometrics 34:305)",
    "pyblp"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 268,
   "method": "Own- and cross-price elasticities and diversion ratios",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/demand_elasticities.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_elasticities",
    "dcd_diversion",
    "dcd_markups"
   ],
   "when": "translating estimated demand parameters into the quantities policy and merger analysis actually use: how much a product loses to a price rise and where that volume goes",
   "when_not": "the demand model has not been estimated yet; you want welfare rather than substitution (-> welfare card)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "diversion ratios",
     "criterion": "the question is where volume goes, not how much is lost"
    },
    {
     "alt": "aggregate elasticity",
     "criterion": "only the market-level response to a uniform price change matters"
    },
    {
     "alt": "logit closed form",
     "criterion": "a plain logit was estimated and the matrix is analytic"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "elasticities: the full own/cross matrix, own on the diagonal",
    "diversion: the diversion-ratio matrix, rows summing to at most one with the outside good",
    "aggregate_elasticity: market-level response to a uniform price change",
    "markups: implied Lerner indices under the assumed conduct"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "heatmap",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the elasticity matrix is NOT symmetric -- element (i,j) is the response of i to the price of j, and reading it transposed inverts every conclusion",
    "diversion ratios exclude the outside good by default; rows that do not sum to one are not an error",
    "elasticities are evaluated at a point; with heterogeneous consumers they vary across markets and the average is not the elasticity of the average"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Nevo 2001 (Econometrica 69:307-342)",
    "Conlon & Mortimer 2021 (American Economic Journal: Microeconomics 13:228)",
    "pyblp"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 269,
   "method": "Merger simulation and counterfactual pricing",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/merger_simulation.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_merger_simulate",
    "dcd_upp",
    "dcd_welfare_change"
   ],
   "when": "predicting the price and welfare effect of a change in ownership or of a cost shock, holding demand and conduct fixed",
   "when_not": "demand is not credibly estimated; entry, repositioning or capacity responses dominate and the fixed-conduct assumption is untenable",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "upward pricing pressure",
     "criterion": "a fast screen that needs no full equilibrium solve"
    },
    {
     "alt": "full merger simulation",
     "criterion": "you need predicted prices and welfare, not a screen"
    },
    {
     "alt": "bargaining models",
     "criterion": "prices are negotiated rather than posted"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "prices_post: the counterfactual equilibrium price vector",
    "delta_prices: change from the observed equilibrium, per product",
    "consumer_surplus_change, profit_change: welfare decomposition",
    "converged: whether the price fixed point actually solved"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the result is conditional on conduct AND on demand; a merger simulation inherits every error in the demand estimate and adds none of its own uncertainty to the reported interval",
    "efficiencies enter as a marginal-cost change and are an ASSUMPTION, not an estimate -- the simulation cannot tell you whether they are real",
    "a non-converged fixed point can still return a plausible-looking price vector; check the flag, not the numbers"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Werden & Froeb 1994 (Journal of Law, Economics and Organization 10:407)",
    "Nevo 2000 (Journal of Economics and Management Strategy 9:513)",
    "Farrell & Shapiro 2010 (BE Journal of Theoretical Economics 10:9)",
    "pyblp"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 270,
   "method": "Nested logit demand",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/nested_logit.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_nested_logit_fit",
    "dcd_nested_logit_elasticities"
   ],
   "when": "a known product grouping captures substitution well enough, and you want a closed-form estimator that runs in seconds rather than a full random-coefficients search",
   "when_not": "the nesting structure is unknown or contested; substitution crosses the nests in ways the structure cannot express (-> BLP)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "plain logit",
     "criterion": "no grouping is needed and IIA is acceptable"
    },
    {
     "alt": "BLP random coefficients",
     "criterion": "substitution is driven by continuous characteristics, not a discrete grouping"
    },
    {
     "alt": "two-level nested logit",
     "criterion": "there is a credible hierarchy of groupings"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "params: characteristic coefficients and the nesting parameter rho",
    "rho: within-nest correlation; must lie in [0,1) for the model to be consistent with utility maximisation",
    "elasticities: closed-form own and cross elasticities",
    "loglike, aic"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "rho outside [0,1) means the estimates are NOT consistent with random utility maximisation -- it is a specification failure, not a large parameter",
    "the within-nest share is mechanically correlated with the error, so it must be instrumented; ignoring that biases rho toward one",
    "the nesting structure is an assumption the data cannot test from inside the model"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Berry 1994 (RAND Journal of Economics 25:242-262)",
    "Cardell 1997 (Econometric Theory 13:185)",
    "Train 2009, Discrete Choice Methods with Simulation",
    "pyblp"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 271,
   "method": "Multinomial and conditional logit",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/multinomial_conditional_logit.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_mnlogit_fit",
    "dcd_clogit_fit",
    "dcd_iia_test"
   ],
   "when": "individual-level choice among a small unordered set, where the regressors vary by chooser (multinomial) or by alternative (conditional)",
   "when_not": "the alternatives are ordered (-> ordered choice); IIA is untenable and you need substitution structure (-> mixed or nested logit); shares are market level",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "mixed logit",
     "criterion": "IIA fails and you have repeated choices or panel structure"
    },
    {
     "alt": "nested logit",
     "criterion": "a discrete grouping captures the substitution"
    },
    {
     "alt": "ordered logit",
     "criterion": "the alternatives have a natural order"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "params: coefficients, per alternative for multinomial and shared for conditional",
    "marginal_effects: probability derivatives, which are what is interpretable",
    "predicted_probabilities: fitted choice probabilities",
    "loglike, aic, pseudo_r2, hausman_iia"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "multinomial logit coefficients are relative to a BASE alternative and change entirely if the base changes; only the marginal effects are invariant",
    "IIA is a substantive restriction, not a technicality -- the Hausman-McFadden test is low power and passing it is weak evidence",
    "a perfectly predicted alternative drives its coefficient to infinity; the optimiser reports a large number rather than an error"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "McFadden 1974, Conditional logit analysis of qualitative choice behavior (Frontiers in Econometrics)",
    "Hausman & McFadden 1984 (Econometrica 52:1219)",
    "statsmodels.discrete"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 272,
   "method": "Mixed and random-parameters logit for panel choice data",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/mixed_logit.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_mixed_logit_fit",
    "dcd_mixed_logit_individual"
   ],
   "when": "repeated choices by the same individuals, or taste heterogeneity you want to estimate rather than assume away; relaxes IIA without imposing a nesting structure",
   "when_not": "you have one choice per person and no basis for heterogeneity; market-level shares only (-> BLP)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "conditional logit",
     "criterion": "no heterogeneity and IIA is acceptable"
    },
    {
     "alt": "latent class logit",
     "criterion": "heterogeneity is discrete rather than continuous"
    },
    {
     "alt": "BLP",
     "criterion": "you observe shares rather than individual choices"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "params: mean and standard deviation of each random coefficient",
    "individual_params: posterior means per chooser, where a panel identifies them",
    "wtp: willingness to pay when a price coefficient is present",
    "loglike, aic, n_draws: the simulation size the estimate rests on"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the estimate is SIMULATED; too few draws produce a likelihood surface with spurious local optima, and the reported standard error does not include simulation noise",
    "a normally distributed price coefficient puts mass on the wrong sign; log-normal or triangular is usually the defensible choice",
    "a random coefficient whose estimated standard deviation is insignificant should be made fixed -- leaving it in inflates every other standard error"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Train 2009, Discrete Choice Methods with Simulation, 2nd ed.",
    "Revelt & Train 1998 (Review of Economics and Statistics 80:647)",
    "McFadden & Train 2000 (Journal of Applied Econometrics 15:447)",
    "xlogit"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 273,
   "method": "Willingness to pay, compensating variation and consumer welfare from discrete choice",
   "wrapper_file": "c31_discrete_choice_demand/choice_welfare.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "dcd_wtp",
    "dcd_compensating_variation",
    "dcd_consumer_surplus"
   ],
   "when": "converting estimated demand into money: what a characteristic is worth, and what a price or choice-set change does to consumer surplus",
   "when_not": "the price coefficient is insignificant or wrong-signed, which makes every money-metric meaningless; you want producer welfare only",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "elasticities",
     "criterion": "the question is substitution rather than money"
    },
    {
     "alt": "log-sum / inclusive value",
     "criterion": "you want the exact closed-form surplus under logit"
    },
    {
     "alt": "simulation-based CV",
     "criterion": "the model has random coefficients and no closed form"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "wtp: characteristic coefficient divided by the price coefficient, with a delta-method interval",
    "compensating_variation: per-chooser money-metric change",
    "consumer_surplus_change: aggregate change, in the price numeraire",
    "log_sum: the inclusive value before and after"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "WTP is a RATIO of estimates, so its distribution is heavy-tailed and the delta-method interval understates uncertainty when the price coefficient is imprecise",
    "surplus is only defined up to the marginal utility of income; comparisons across specifications with different price coefficients are not comparable",
    "with random coefficients the mean of the WTP ratio is not the ratio of the means, and is often undefined"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Small & Rosen 1981 (Econometrica 49:105-130)",
    "Train 2009, Discrete Choice Methods with Simulation, ch.3",
    "Daly, Hess & de Jong 2012 (Transportation Research Part B 46:333)",
    "pyblp"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  }
 ],
